    "math_academy_id": "q-55433",
    "knowledge_point_id": "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931806/assets/2d0b1cdb3ff99abaab113c5b0beaf0e1a3ad8738cbd84b1f95da5b7c1139582b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$  Find the value of $k-c.$",
    "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ hence $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =3 \\\\ k-3 & =3 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=6-(-3)=9.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=6{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$",
